    {
      "id": "JAhr2hEQc3xC2Geqn-zAD",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 10633.033450989416,
      "y": 13049.56523981795,
      "width": 271.7281188964844,
      "height": 45,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bwp",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 889688239,
      "version": 32,
      "versionNonce": 1825871937,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789830627912,
      "created": 1789827393042,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "146. LRU Cache",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "146. LRU Cache",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "UY-CeP7PSInvLu_2VS_va",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 10661.754398896646,
      "y": 13114.254093571304,
      "width": 1416.689158957942,
      "height": 2430,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bxz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 775607073,
      "version": 4117,
      "versionNonce": 1524467501,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789874271427,
      "created": 1789831038370,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Case: Out of Capacity\n==> Check the least recently usage\n--> need 2nd datastructure, why?\n\nWhen we get a specific key, we likely \"used\" that key, therefor the\norder/priority is changed. The map itself cannot handle this logic so we have to\nuse another datastructure for this.\n\nWhen a key is accessed, we have to reorder the stored keys, but usually,\nreorder would coupling to sorting. In this case using the array is not a good\nidea since the problem required that all method should produce O(1) time\ncomplexity.\n\nWe might think another way, like the Insert/Remove/GetRandom O(1) problem,\nwe used a map with pair constitute with {val - array_index} and the array for\nstoring values. We not iterating for removing the val, we have O(1) lookup for\nvalues' index by virtue of the map, and we can also have O(1) removal by\nswapping that specific val to the last index and then shrink the array by 1. The\nquestion is, can we reuse that design for this LRU cache? \n\n## get(key int)\nEasily O(1) for get by using map and array. But this is the LRU cache, we have\nto update the prioty of the array and the new index within the map also.\n\nfor example we have: \n    - array: [1,3,4,5] --> value\n    - map: [{1-0}, {3-1}, {4-2}, {5-3}] --> key\n\nwhen we get(4), we have to:\n    1. update array: [1,2,5,4] - with the assumpton that the left most one is\nLRU and the right most one is MRU\n    2. Update new index for the map: [{1-0}, {3-1}, {4-3}, {5-2}] \n\n\n## put (key int, val int)\nappend to the end of the array and add new key-index to the map. \n\nwhen out of capacity, we remove the Left most value within the array, also in\nthe map and start to append like usual.\n---\n\nI have not seen no problem with array. Let's try it first.\n\nI saw the problem now. The inferiority of the array is the amount of\nattributes it can store. when we put a pair key-value obey the requirement, I\nsimply misunderstood that the array value within the array will equal with the\nkey - like [1,1] [4,4]. but if we have the [2,4] pair the swapping logic will be\nderailed since after we swapped we wont able to access back to the map. We\nonly store the key with index, and val is at that index. but when we swap, the\nval is no longer stay at the index and also we have no clue how to get back the\nkey paired with that value also. \n---\n\nBack to doubly linked list again. ",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Case: Out of Capacity\n==> Check the least recently usage\n--> need 2nd datastructure, why?\n\nWhen we get a specific key, we likely \"used\" that key, therefor the order/priority is changed. The map itself cannot handle this logic so we have to use another datastructure for this.\n\nWhen a key is accessed, we have to reorder the stored keys, but usually, reorder would coupling to sorting. In this case using the array is not a good idea since the problem required that all method should produce O(1) time complexity.\n\nWe might think another way, like the Insert/Remove/GetRandom O(1) problem, we used a map with pair constitute with {val - array_index} and the array for storing values. We not iterating for removing the val, we have O(1) lookup for values' index by virtue of the map, and we can also have O(1) removal by swapping that specific val to the last index and then shrink the array by 1. The question is, can we reuse that design for this LRU cache? \n\n## get(key int)\nEasily O(1) for get by using map and array. But this is the LRU cache, we have to update the prioty of the array and the new index within the map also.\n\nfor example we have: \n    - array: [1,3,4,5] --> value\n    - map: [{1-0}, {3-1}, {4-2}, {5-3}] --> key\n\nwhen we get(4), we have to:\n    1. update array: [1,2,5,4] - with the assumpton that the left most one is LRU and the right most one is MRU\n    2. Update new index for the map: [{1-0}, {3-1}, {4-3}, {5-2}] \n\n\n## put (key int, val int)\nappend to the end of the array and add new key-index to the map. \n\nwhen out of capacity, we remove the Left most value within the array, also in the map and start to append like usual.\n---\n\nI have not seen no problem with array. Let's try it first.\n\nI saw the problem now. The inferiority of the array is the amount of attributes it can store. when we put a pair key-value obey the requirement, I simply misunderstood that the array value within the array will equal with the key - like [1,1] [4,4]. but if we have the [2,4] pair the swapping logic will be derailed since after we swapped we wont able to access back to the map. We only store the key with index, and val is at that index. but when we swap, the val is no longer stay at the index and also we have no clue how to get back the key paired with that value also. \n---\n\nBack to doubly linked list again. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "ZE0vI_2NsPdHrliNdpdF8",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 12249.337354777474,
      "y": 13097.106331452276,
      "width": 1418.3928920200883,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "by0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1339988527,
      "version": 1795,
      "versionNonce": 1029577101,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789874271427,
      "created": 1789831294830,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "With doubly Linked-list. The map will now storing the key paired with the Node.\nwhich mean when we swap the nodes within the list, we dont have to update\nthe map since the value is a pointer.\n\nStill preserve the advantage of the map, we quickly get the node with the key.\nbut instead of swap, we remove and reinsert it at the end of the list. \n\nTo achieve O(1), the Insert and Remove method of the linkedlist should\nproduce O(1) also. To make this able, we have to store the head and tail, with\nakin to the idea of using array - we storeing the lru and mru respectively left\nmode value and right most value. Insert and Remove will compute based on\nthose two node only. \n\nThe out of capacity is still easy to maintain, by just modify the lru node\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "With doubly Linked-list. The map will now storing the key paired with the Node. which mean when we swap the nodes within the list, we dont have to update the map since the value is a pointer.\n\nStill preserve the advantage of the map, we quickly get the node with the key. but instead of swap, we remove and reinsert it at the end of the list. \n\nTo achieve O(1), the Insert and Remove method of the linkedlist should produce O(1) also. To make this able, we have to store the head and tail, with akin to the idea of using array - we storeing the lru and mru respectively left mode value and right most value. Insert and Remove will compute based on those two node only. \n\nThe out of capacity is still easy to maintain, by just modify the lru node\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
